            <ArticleTitle>Brucella Causing Liver Abscess in a Child with Selective IgA Deficiency.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>595-596</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Brucella has been known to cause pyrexia of unknown origin.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE CHARACTERISTICS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">9-year-old boy with fever and abdominal pain; multiple abscesses within the liver on ultrasonography.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBSERVATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">IgM Antibodies against Brucella were raised in his serum sample, and Brucella serum agglutination test was positive. Immunological work-up suggested selective IgA deficiency. Reduction in size following treatment with trimethoprim-sulphamethoxazole, amikacin and doxycycline.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MESSAGE" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Brucellosis should be considered as an etiology of liver abscess in patients not responding to conventional antibiotics.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
